    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly, for the particular issue of high concentrations of organic vapors and heavier aerosols, you'd generally want an industrial-grade air purification system. These are similar in principle to those used in cleanrooms and might be a bit more technical and expensive to set up in a home.\n\n1. Photoionization (PI) Air Purifiers - These can neutralize airborne chemicals and germs by converting them into charged particles that can then be more easily captured by a filter. I'm not suggesting specific models here, as they can be complex and may not be practical for a residential setting, but they're worth researching.\n\n2. Ozone Generating Air Purifiers - They do produce ozone, which can chemically neutralize organic vapors. Given the potential health risks of ozone, I'd strongly recommend consulting with a healthcare provider before using one.\n\n3. Ultraviolet Germicidal Irradiation (UVGI) systems - These are effective against a wide range of airborne pathogens and can break down organic molecules. UVGI systems with copper coils can also decompose some hydrocarbons, but they're not all-encompassing and can sometimes re-release particulate matter that was just neutralized.\n\nAs for sealing your windows and vents, there are several steps you can take:\n\n1. Weather",
